/**
 * `unsafe` on a STRUCTURAL alias — `type Danger = ptr<byte>`, with no body and no methods of its
 * own. Rejected (AC#4, cargo/20-unsafe-alias-rejeitado): before this gate, `unsafe` here compiled
 * and did NOTHING — `Danger` resolves straight THROUGH to `ptr<byte>` (`resolve.tks:794`) and its
 * own name never survives resolution, so `unsafe_carrying_at`'s `Named` lookup (`resolve.tks:1158`)
 * never found the stamp. A SAFE-looking wrapper could carry it past the contagion gate untouched —
 * exactly the silent hole `docs/design/c-types-and-marshalling-0.3.1.md` §9.2 names.
 *
 * The rejection comes from `reject_unsafe_alias` (`src/checker/collect.tks`), called by
 * `validate_one_type_decl`'s `AliasBody` arm — a SOFT pre-walk pass: it collects this diagnostic
 * and the build carries on to report every other declaration's too (issue #68), so this case is a
 * NAMESPACE of the shared failing build rather than a `cases/*.tks` paying for its own.
 *
 * @example expected diagnostic: type alias 'Danger' cannot be declared `unsafe`
 */
pub unsafe type Danger = ptr<byte>
